    {TEXT("这里发生了首次有记录的坦克对战。"), TEXT("The first recorded tank-versus-tank battle took place here."), TEXT("The first recorded tank-versus-tank battle took place here.")},
    {TEXT("阵中日记 · 亚眠以东 · 一九一八年八月八日\n\n低雾遮住村界，战车从前队身旁驶过。澳大利亚与加拿大部队已经出发，炮火向远处延伸。步枪手越过田埂，隔着雾寻找相邻队伍的身影。\n\n传令兵在岔路留下指路的人，携弹队沿车辙跟来。孤立枪位还在抵抗，前面时而有人停下卧倒。发动机的声音渐远，一辆战车只剩露出雾面的车顶。\n\n鲁登道夫称这一天为德国陆军的黑暗之日。"), TEXT("WAR DIARY · East of Amiens · Eight August nineteen eighteen\n\nLow mist hides the village boundary. A tank passes the leading infantry. Australian and Canadian troops are moving forward as the barrage lifts into the distance. Riflemen cross the field bank and search through the mist for the next party.\n\nA runner leaves a man at the fork to guide the bearers following the tracks. Isolated posts still resist. Men ahead stop and lie down when shots reach them. Engine noise fades until only a tank's roof shows above the mist.\n\nLudendorff called it the German Army's black day."), TEXT("一九一八年八月八日清晨，协约国在低雾中进攻亚眠以东。澳大利亚与加拿大部队推进，英军位于北侧，法军在更南一侧。炮兵、坦克和飞机共同支援，打击德军前沿及增援交通。\n\n本连随初次炮火穿过田野与受损村界，步兵越过孤立哨位，坦克沿接近路继续移动。携弹队从道路跟进，传令兵维持邻队联系。雾和幸存德军阵位仍遮断视野，各支部队无法直接看清整段推进前线。")},
    {TEXT("阵中日记 · 亚眠以东 · 一九一八年八月八日\n\n低雾遮住村界，战车从前队身旁驶过。澳大利亚与加拿大部队已经出发，炮火向远处延伸。步枪手越过田埂，隔着雾寻找相邻队伍的身影。\n\n传令兵在岔路留下指路的人，携弹队沿车辙跟来。孤立枪位还在抵抗，前面时而有人停下卧倒。发动机的声音渐远，一辆战车只剩露出雾面的车顶。\n\n鲁登道夫称这一天为德国陆军的黑暗之日。"), TEXT("WAR DIARY · East of Amiens · Eight August nineteen eighteen\n\nLow mist hides the village boundary. A tank passes the leading infantry. Australian and Canadian troops are moving forward as the barrage lifts into the distance. Riflemen cross the field bank and search through the mist for the next party.\n\nA runner leaves a man at the fork to guide the bearers following the tracks. Isolated posts still resist. Men ahead stop and lie down when shots reach them. Engine noise fades until only a tank's roof shows above the mist.\n\nLudendorff called it the German Army's black day."), TEXT("At dawn on 8 August 1918, Allied forces attack east of Amiens under low mist. Australian and Canadian formations advance with British troops to the north and French troops farther south. Artillery, tanks and aircraft support the operation together, disrupting the German forward positions and reinforcements.\n\nThe company moves through fields and damaged village boundaries behind the opening fire. Infantry pass isolated posts while tanks continue along the approaches. Ammunition parties follow the roads, and runners maintain contact between neighbouring units. Mist and surviving German positions still interrupt observation across the advancing front.")},
